2	1	Chemistry	Energetics	simple	{"summary": "Energetics in chemistry deals with the study of energy changes, especially heat, during chemical reactions. Key concepts include exothermic and endothermic reactions, enthalpy changes ($\\\\Delta H$), and simple calculations like heat of reaction.", "detailed_explanation": "Energetics focuses on how energy, especially heat, is absorbed or released during chemical processes. When a chemical reaction occurs, bonds are broken and new ones are formed. Breaking bonds requires energy (endothermic), while forming bonds releases energy (exothermic). The overall heat change in a reaction is called the enthalpy change ($\\\\Delta H$).\\n\\n- **Exothermic reactions**: These release heat to the surroundings. The temperature of the surroundings increases. Example: Combustion of fuels like methane ($CH_4 + 2O_2 \\\\rightarrow CO_2 + 2H_2O$). Here, $\\\\Delta H$ is negative.\\n- **Endothermic reactions**: These absorb heat from the surroundings. The temperature of the surroundings decreases. Example: Dissolving ammonium chloride in water ($NH_4Cl(s) \\\\rightarrow NH_4^+(aq) + Cl^-(aq)$). Here, $\\\\Delta H$ is positive.\\n\\nWe often calculate the heat energy change ($q$) using the formula:\\n\\n$$q = mc\\\\Delta\\\\theta$$\\n\\nWhere:\\n- $m$ = mass of substance (in grams)\\n- $c$ = specific heat capacity (in J/g^\\\\circ C)\\n- $\\\\Delta\\\\theta$ = change in temperature (in \\\\circ C)\\n\\n\\n| Reaction Type    | $\\\\Delta H$ (sign) | Heat Flow             | Example                                 |\\n|------------------|-------------------|-----------------------|------------------------------------------|\\n| Exothermic       | Negative ($-$)    | Released to surroundings | Combustion of methane ($CH_4$)           |\\n| Endothermic      | Positive ($+$)    | Absorbed from surroundings | Dissolving $NH_4Cl$ in water            |\\n\\n**Diagram description**:\\n- Enthalpy level diagram: For exothermic reactions, the products are lower than reactants; for endothermic, products are higher.\\n\\n**Real-world relevance**: Cooking food (exothermic), cold packs for injuries (endothermic), burning fuels for energy (exothermic).", "examples": ["Example: Combustion of methane. Problem: Is the combustion of methane exothermic or endothermic? Solution: $CH_4 + 2O_2 \\\\rightarrow CO_2 + 2H_2O$ releases heat, so it is exothermic ($\\\\Delta H < 0$).", "Example: Calculate the heat change when 100 g of water is heated from 25$^\\\\circ$C to 35$^\\\\circ$C. Solution: $q = mc\\\\Delta\\\\theta = 100 \\\\times 4.2 \\\\times (35-25) = 4200$ J"], "practice_questions": ["State whether the following reaction is exothermic or endothermic: $NaOH(s) + H_2O(l) \\\\rightarrow Na^+(aq) + OH^-(aq)$ (Temperature rises).", "Draw and label a simple enthalpy level diagram for an exothermic reaction.", "Calculate the heat absorbed when 50 g of water is cooled from 60$^\\\\circ$C to 30$^\\\\circ$C. (Specific heat capacity of water $= 4.2$ J/g$^\\\\circ$C)."], "key_concepts": ["Exothermic and endothermic reactions", "Enthalpy change ($\\\\Delta H$)", "Energy level diagrams", "Heat of reaction calculations"], "common_mistakes": ["Confusing exothermic and endothermic reactions (mixing up heat absorbed and released).", "Forgetting to use the correct signs for $\\\\Delta H$.", "Not converting all units properly (e.g., grams to kilograms)."], "exam_tips": ["Always state whether $\\\\Delta H$ is positive or negative for exothermic or endothermic reactions.", "Pay attention to the direction of heat flow when answering questions (ask: is heat entering or leaving the system?)."]}	GD4INHJLPPk	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GD4INHJLPPk	2026-01-14 12:14:35.715763+01	\N	2026-01-14 12:14:35.715763+01
3	1	Mathematics	Number Bases	simple	{"summary": "Number bases show how numbers are written using different sets of digits and place values. The most common base is decimal (base 10), but WAEC/JAMB often ask questions about converting between bases like binary (base 2), octal (base 8), and hexadecimal (base 16).", "detailed_explanation": "A number base (also called radix) tells you how many unique digits are used to count. For example, in base 10 (decimal), we use digits 0\\u20139. In base 2 (binary), only 0 and 1 are used. Each position in a number has a value that is a power of the base.\\n\\nFor example, the decimal number $253$ means:\\n\\n$$\\n253 = 2 \\\\times 10^2 + 5 \\\\times 10^1 + 3 \\\\times 10^0\\n$$\\n\\nIn base 2 (binary), the number $1011_2$ means:\\n\\n$$\\n1011_2 = 1 \\\\times 2^3 + 0 \\\\times 2^2 + 1 \\\\times 2^1 + 1 \\\\times 2^0 = 8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = 11_{10}\\n$$\\n\\nTo convert from base 10 to another base, divide the number by the new base and keep track of the remainders. To convert from another base to base 10, expand using powers of the base.\\n\\n**Table: Number Base Comparison**\\n\\n| Base Name     | Base | Digits Used      | Example    |\\n|--------------|------|-----------------|------------|\\n| Binary       | 2    | 0, 1            | $1011_2$   |\\n| Octal        | 8    | 0-7             | $127_8$    |\\n| Decimal      | 10   | 0-9             | $253_{10}$ |\\n| Hexadecimal  | 16   | 0-9, A-F        | $1A3_{16}$ |\\n\\n**Diagram Description**: Imagine place values as columns, where each column's value increases by powers of the base (e.g., in base 2: 8, 4, 2, 1; in base 10: 100, 10, 1). This helps when converting and understanding how numbers are built in different bases.", "examples": ["Example 1: Convert $1010_2$ to base 10.\\n\\n$1010_2 = 1 \\\\times 2^3 + 0 \\\\times 2^2 + 1 \\\\times 2^1 + 0 \\\\times 2^0 = 8 + 0 + 2 + 0 = 10_{10}$\\n\\nExample 2: Convert $45_{10}$ to base 2.\\n\\n$45 \\\\div 2 = 22$ remainder $1$\\n$22 \\\\div 2 = 11$ remainder $0$\\n$11 \\\\div 2 = 5$ remainder $1$\\n$5 \\\\div 2 = 2$ remainder $1$\\n$2 \\\\div 2 = 1$ remainder $0$\\n$1 \\\\div 2 = 0$ remainder $1$\\n\\nWriting the remainders in reverse: $101101_2$\\n\\nSo, $45_{10} = 101101_2$", "Example 3: Convert $237_8$ to base 10.\\n\\n$237_8 = 2 \\\\times 8^2 + 3 \\\\times 8^1 + 7 \\\\times 8^0 = 128 + 24 + 7 = 159_{10}$"], "practice_questions": ["Convert $1101_2$ to base 10.", "Convert $56_{10}$ to base 2.", "Convert $89_{10}$ to base 8.", "Convert $2F_{16}$ to base 10 (A=10, B=11, ..., F=15).", "Express $275_{10}$ in base 5."], "key_concepts": ["Number base (radix)", "Place value and powers of the base", "Converting between bases (to and from base 10)"], "common_mistakes": ["Forgetting to write remainders in reverse order when converting from base 10 to another base.", "Using the wrong powers of the base when expanding a number.", "Mixing up which base you are working in or reading the subscript incorrectly."], "exam_tips": ["Always write the base as a subscript to avoid confusion ($101_2$, $101_{10}$, etc).", "Show all steps clearly when converting between bases; examiners give marks for correct method even if final answer is wrong.", "Practice base conversion regularly, especially with binary and octal, as these are common in WAEC/JAMB."]}	QpZIUDBMhg8	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QpZIUDBMhg8	2026-01-14 12:23:06.932395+01	\N	2026-01-14 12:23:06.932395+01
4	1	Civic Education	Citizenship	medium	{"summary": "Citizenship refers to the legal status and identity of a person recognized as a member of a country, with specific rights and responsibilities. In Nigeria, citizenship can be acquired by birth, registration, or naturalization as provided by the 1999 Constitution.", "detailed_explanation": "Citizenship is the legal bond between an individual and a country, which confers rights (such as voting, freedom of movement) and duties (like obeying laws, paying taxes). In Nigeria, citizenship is defined by the 1999 Constitution and can be acquired in three main ways:\\n\\n1. **By Birth:** A person born in Nigeria to Nigerian parents or to parents who are indigenes of any Nigerian community automatically acquires Nigerian citizenship.\\n\\n2. **By Registration:** This applies mainly to non-Nigerian women married to Nigerian men, or people of Nigerian descent born abroad who wish to acquire Nigerian citizenship.\\n\\n3. **By Naturalization:** Foreigners who have lived in Nigeria for a specific period and meet certain requirements (such as good character and intention to reside permanently) may apply for Nigerian citizenship.\\n\\nRights of citizens include voting and being voted for, freedom of speech, freedom of religion, and access to public services. Duties include obedience to the law, payment of taxes, and defending Nigeria when necessary.\\n\\n| Mode of Acquisition | Who Is Eligible | Example |\\n|--------------------|----------------|---------|\\n| By Birth           | Born in Nigeria to Nigerian parents | A child born in Lagos to Yoruba parents |\\n| By Registration    | Foreign women married to Nigerians, or people of Nigerian descent abroad | A Ghanaian woman married to a Nigerian man |\\n| By Naturalization  | Foreigners meeting residency and other criteria | A German living in Nigeria for 15 years, applies for citizenship |\\n\\n*Diagram description:* Imagine a Venn diagram showing three overlapping circles labeled 'Birth', 'Registration', and 'Naturalization', with 'Nigerian Citizen' at the center where they overlap.", "examples": ["Example problem with solution:\\nProblem: State two differences between citizenship by birth and citizenship by naturalization in Nigeria.\\nSolution:\\n- By birth: Automatically acquired by being born to Nigerian parents in Nigeria.\\n- By naturalization: Acquired by foreigners who have lived in Nigeria for a set period and fulfill legal requirements."], "practice_questions": ["Define citizenship and list three ways it can be acquired in Nigeria.", "Using a markdown table, compare the rights of citizens with the duties of citizens.", "Explain the difference between citizenship by registration and by naturalization."], "key_concepts": ["Citizenship", "Rights and Duties", "Acquisition of Citizenship (Birth, Registration, Naturalization)", "Loss of Citizenship"], "common_mistakes": ["Confusing citizenship by birth with citizenship by registration.", "Assuming every person born in Nigeria is automatically a citizen, regardless of their parents' nationality."], "exam_tips": ["Always use examples from the Nigerian context when explaining types of citizenship.", "For questions asking for differences or comparisons, use tables or clear bullet points for clarity."]}	x7fMGvb4mkk	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x7fMGvb4mkk	2026-01-14 12:34:45.176885+01	\N	2026-01-14 12:34:45.176885+01
5	1	Physics	Physical Quantities & Units	simple	{"summary": "Physical quantities are properties in physics that can be measured, such as length, mass, and time. Each physical quantity is measured in a specific unit, like metre ($m$) for length, kilogram ($kg$) for mass, and second ($s$) for time.", "detailed_explanation": "In physics, we often talk about things that we can measure\\u2014these are called physical quantities. Examples include length, mass, time, temperature, and electric current. Each physical quantity has its own unit, which is a standard way of expressing how much of that quantity we have. The International System of Units (SI units) is used worldwide, including in WAEC/JAMB exams in Nigeria. \\n\\nThere are two types of physical quantities:\\n\\n1. **Fundamental quantities**: These are basic quantities that cannot be broken down into simpler ones. Examples: length, mass, time, electric current, thermodynamic temperature, amount of substance, and luminous intensity.\\n2. **Derived quantities**: These are obtained by combining fundamental quantities. Examples: velocity (distance/time), area (length \\u00d7 length), and density (mass/volume).\\n\\nBelow is a table showing some fundamental quantities, their symbols, and their SI units:\\n\\n| Quantity               | Symbol | SI Unit       | Unit Symbol |\\n|------------------------|--------|---------------|-------------|\\n| Length                 | $l$    | metre         | $m$         |\\n| Mass                   | $m$    | kilogram      | $kg$        |\\n| Time                   | $t$    | second        | $s$         |\\n| Electric current       | $I$    | ampere        | $A$         |\\n| Thermodynamic temperature | $T$ | kelvin        | $K$         |\\n| Amount of substance    | $n$    | mole          | $mol$       |\\n| Luminous intensity     | $Iv$   | candela       | $cd$        |\\n\\n\\n**Diagram description:** Imagine a table with columns for quantity, symbol, SI unit, and unit symbol\\u2014this helps you quickly match a physical quantity to its correct SI unit.\\n\\n**Why is this important?**\\nWAEC/JAMB questions often ask you to identify correct units, convert between units, or distinguish between fundamental and derived quantities.", "examples": ["Example problem: What is the SI unit of mass? Solution: The SI unit of mass is kilogram ($kg$).", "Example problem: Density is defined as mass per unit volume. What is its SI unit? Solution: Density = mass/volume. SI unit = $kg/m^3$.", "Example comparison table:\\n| Quantity | Type         | SI Unit |\\n|----------|--------------|---------|\\n| Area     | Derived      | $m^2$   |\\n| Volume   | Derived      | $m^3$   |\\n| Force    | Derived      | $N$ (newton) |"], "practice_questions": ["State two examples each of fundamental and derived quantities.", "Give the SI units for length, time, and electric current.", "Convert 250 cm to metres. (Use: $1 m = 100 cm$)", "What is the SI unit of force?"], "key_concepts": ["Physical quantities", "SI units", "Fundamental and derived quantities"], "common_mistakes": ["Mixing up the units for mass (kg) and weight (N).", "Using non-SI units like gram (g) instead of kilogram (kg) or minute (min) instead of second (s) in exam answers."], "exam_tips": ["Memorise the seven fundamental quantities and their SI units\\u2014they are frequently tested.", "Always use SI units in calculations and answers, unless the question specifically asks for another unit."]}	iXfCIDmulwo	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iXfCIDmulwo	2026-01-14 12:38:14.883895+01	\N	2026-01-14 12:38:14.883895+01
1	1	Physics	Scalars & Vectors	simple	{"summary": "Scalars are quantities with only magnitude (size), such as mass or temperature, while vectors have both magnitude and direction, like velocity or force. In WAEC/JAMB exams, you must recognize the difference and perform simple operations with vectors using diagrams and calculations.", "detailed_explanation": "In physics, quantities are categorized as either scalars or vectors depending on whether they have only magnitude or both magnitude and direction.\\n\\n**Scalars**: These are physical quantities described only by their size (magnitude), e.g., $5 \\\\\\\\ \\\\mathrm{kg}$ of rice or $12 \\\\\\\\ \\\\mathrm{m}$ of rope. Common scalar quantities include mass, time, speed, temperature, and distance.\\n\\n**Vectors**: These quantities have both magnitude and direction, e.g., a force of $10 \\\\\\\\ \\\\mathrm{N}$ to the east. Examples of vectors include displacement, velocity, acceleration, and force.\\n\\nYou can represent vectors visually with arrows. The length shows the magnitude, and the arrow points in the direction.\\n\\n### Comparison Table\\n| Quantity       | Scalar or Vector | Example               |\\n|----------------|------------------|-----------------------|\\n| Distance       | Scalar           | $15 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{km}$         |\\n| Displacement   | Vector           | $8 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{km}$ north    |\\n| Speed          | Scalar           | $40 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{m/s}$        |\\n| Velocity       | Vector           | $40 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{m/s}$ east   |\\n| Mass           | Scalar           | $2 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{kg}$          |\\n| Force          | Vector           | $5 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{N}$ upward    |\\n\\n#### Diagrams (Described)\\nImagine drawing an arrow 4 cm long pointing east. If 1 cm represents $5 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{N}$, then the arrow represents a $20 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{N}$ force to the east. For scalars, you just write the number with the unit\\u2014no direction is needed.\\n\\n#### Operations with Vectors\\n- **Adding Vectors (Triangle or Parallelogram Rule):** Place vectors tip-to-tail; the resultant is from start to finish.\\n- **Example:** Two forces, $3 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{N}$ north and $4 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{N}$ east, combine to give a resultant using Pythagoras' theorem:\\n  $$\\n  R = \\\\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{N}\\n  $$\\n  The direction is found using trigonometry.\\n\\n- **Scalars** simply add or subtract without considering direction.\\n", "examples": ["Example 1: Identify if the following are scalars or vectors: (a) $25 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{m}$ (b) $5 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{m/s}$ west.\\nSolution: (a) Scalar (distance), (b) Vector (velocity, has direction).", "Example 2: Two forces act on a point: $4 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{N}$ east and $3 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{N}$ north. Find the magnitude of the resultant force.\\nSolution: $R = \\\\sqrt{4^2 + 3^2} = \\\\sqrt{16 + 9} = \\\\sqrt{25} = 5 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{N}$."], "practice_questions": ["State whether each of the following quantities is a scalar or a vector: (a) temperature, (b) velocity, (c) mass, (d) displacement.", "If you walk $6 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{km}$ east, then $8 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{km}$ north, what is your displacement from the starting point (magnitude only)?", "A car moves at $60 \\\\ \\\\mathrm{km/h}$ north. Is this speed or velocity? Explain.", "Complete the table:\\n| Quantity           | Scalar or Vector | Reason                              |\\n|--------------------|------------------|-------------------------------------|\\n| Work               |                  |                                     |\\n| Acceleration       |                  |                                     |"], "key_concepts": ["Scalar quantities", "Vector quantities", "Magnitude", "Direction", "Vector representation", "Resultant vector"], "common_mistakes": ["Confusing scalar and vector quantities (e.g., thinking speed is a vector).", "Ignoring direction when dealing with vectors.", "Adding vectors like scalars without considering their direction."], "exam_tips": ["Always state both magnitude and direction for vector answers.", "Use simple diagrams to show vector addition or direction in questions.", "Memorize a few common scalar and vector quantities for quick identification in multiple-choice questions."]}	rcDXQ-5H8mk	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rcDXQ-5H8mk	2026-01-14 11:51:34.619494+01	2026-01-14 12:40:55.239834+01	2026-01-14 12:40:55.244045+01
\.


